### What happens when a consumer crashes before committing?

Kafka tracks each consumer group's position per partition as a committed offset stored in the internal offsets topic. A record is only considered done when its offset is committed. So if a consumer polls records at offsets 50 through 59, begins processing, and crashes at offset 55 before committing, the committed offset is still 50. When the group rebalances and another member takes over, it resumes from offset 50 and reprocesses 50 through 59.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class CrashBeforeCommitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "settlement-service");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, "false"); // Manual commit

        try (KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props)) {
            consumer.subscribe(Collections.singletonList("transfers"));

            ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofSeconds(5));
            for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
                System.out.println("Processing: offset=" + record.offset()
                    + " key=" + record.key() + " value=" + record.value());
                // If crash happens HERE -- before commitSync below --
                // the committed offset stays at the previous value
                // and these records will be reprocessed on resume
            }
            consumer.commitSync(); // Commit AFTER processing succeeds
            System.out.println("Offsets committed -- records are done");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Processing: offset=50 key=SAV2001 value=DEBIT 50000
Processing: offset=51 key=SAV3001 value=CREDIT 50000
Offsets committed -- records are done
```

### Why is auto-commit dangerous?

With `enable.auto.commit=true`, the consumer commits offsets automatically every `auto.commit.interval.ms`, by default every five seconds. The danger is that auto-commit can commit offsets for records you have polled but not yet finished processing. If you crash after the auto-commit but before processing, those records are skipped on resume, which is message loss.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class AutoCommitDanger {
    // WRONG: auto-commit risks message loss
    public static Properties wrongConfig() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, "true"); // Default
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.AUTO_COMMIT_INTERVAL_MS_CONFIG, "5000");
        // Timer commits offsets for polled but unprocessed records
        // Crash after auto-commit but before processing = LOSS
        System.out.println("WRONG: auto-commit can lose unprocessed records");
        return props;
    }

    // CORRECT: manual commit after processing
    public static Properties correctConfig() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, "false");
        // Commit only after processing succeeds
        // Crash before commit = safe reprocess (at-least-once)
        System.out.println("CORRECT: manual commit after processing -- at-least-once");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        wrongConfig();
        correctConfig();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: auto-commit can lose unprocessed records
CORRECT: manual commit after processing -- at-least-once
```

### How do you implement reliable at-least-once delivery?

Turn off auto-commit and commit manually after processing succeeds. Poll a batch, process each record fully including any database write, and only then call `commitSync()`.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class AtLeastOnceConsumer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "settlement-service");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, "false");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_RECORDS_CONFIG, "100");

        try (KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props)) {
            consumer.subscribe(Collections.singletonList("transfers"));

            while (true) {
                ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500));
                for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
                    // Step 1: Process the record (database write)
                    settleTransfer(record.key(), record.value());
                }
                if (!records.isEmpty()) {
                    // Step 2: Commit ONLY after all records are processed
                    consumer.commitSync();
                    System.out.println("Committed offsets for " + records.count() + " records");
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private static void settleTransfer(String accountId, String payload) {
        System.out.println("Settled: " + accountId + " -> " + payload);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Settled: SAV2001 -> DEBIT 50000
Settled: SAV3001 -> CREDIT 50000
Committed offsets for 2 records
```

### Why is idempotent processing essential?

Since duplicates are possible with at-least-once, you design the downstream so applying the same record twice has the same effect as once. In banking you use an idempotency key, often the transfer id, and a processed-ids table with a unique constraint.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class IdempotentSettlement {
    // Simulates a processed-transfers table with unique constraint
    private static final Set<String> processedTransfers = new HashSet<>();

    public static boolean settle(String transferId, String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        if (processedTransfers.contains(transferId)) {
            System.out.println("SKIP: Transfer " + transferId + " already processed");
            return false; // Idempotent -- no double charge
        }
        // Apply the settlement
        processedTransfers.add(transferId);
        System.out.println("SETTLED: " + transferId + " account=" + accountId
            + " amount=" + amount);
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // First processing -- succeeds
        settle("TXF-123", "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        // Duplicate after crash and reprocess -- safely skipped
        settle("TXF-123", "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        // Different transfer -- succeeds
        settle("TXF-124", "SAV3001", new BigDecimal("25000"));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
SETTLED: TXF-123 account=SAV2001 amount=50000
SKIP: Transfer TXF-123 already processed
SETTLED: TXF-124 account=SAV3001 amount=25000
```

### What is the difference between commitSync and commitAsync?

`commitSync()` blocks until the commit is acknowledged, giving certainty but adding latency. `commitAsync()` returns immediately with a callback for lower latency but can silently fail. A common pattern is `commitAsync` during normal processing and a final `commitSync` on shutdown or rebalance.

| Method | Behavior | Use Case |
|---|---|---|
| `commitSync()` | Blocks until commit acknowledged | Shutdown, rebalance listener |
| `commitAsync()` | Returns immediately, callback on result | Normal processing loop |

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import java.util.Map;

public class CommitPatternDemo {
    public static void demonstrateCommitPattern(KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer) {
        // Normal loop: async for throughput
        consumer.commitAsync((offsets, exception) -> {
            if (exception != null) {
                System.out.println("Async commit failed: " + exception.getMessage());
            } else {
                System.out.println("Async commit succeeded");
            }
        });

        // On shutdown or rebalance: sync for certainty
        // consumer.commitSync();
    }
}
```

### What is the correct ordering of process, persist, and commit?

The ordering matters for correctness. Committing before persisting is at-most-once and can lose data. Persisting then committing is at-least-once and safe when idempotent.

| Order | Semantics | Risk |
|---|---|---|
| Commit, then process | At-most-once | Crash after commit loses records |
| Process, then commit | At-least-once | Crash before commit reprocesses |
| Process + commit atomically | Exactly-once | Requires Kafka transactions |

### What are common mistakes with offset commit handling?

The most common mistake is persisting to the database and committing the offset in two separate, non-atomic operations. If the commit succeeds but the database write fails, the record is skipped. If the database write succeeds but the commit fails, the record is reprocessed. The fix is to make the consumer idempotent so reprocessing is safe, or to store the offset in the same database transaction as the business data.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class OffsetCommitMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: commit before persist");
        System.out.println("  consumer.commitSync();");
        System.out.println("  database.insert(record); // Fails -> record lost!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: persist then commit (without idempotency)");
        System.out.println("  database.insert(record);");
        System.out.println("  consumer.commitSync(); // Fails -> record reprocessed");
        System.out.println("  // If insert is not idempotent -> duplicate row!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: persist with idempotency key, then commit");
        System.out.println("  database.upsert(record, idempotencyKey);");
        System.out.println("  consumer.commitSync();");
        System.out.println("  // Reprocess is safe: upsert is a no-op on duplicate");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: commit before persist
  consumer.commitSync();
  database.insert(record); // Fails -> record lost!

WRONG: persist then commit (without idempotency)
  database.insert(record);
  consumer.commitSync(); // Fails -> record reprocessed
  // If insert is not idempotent -> duplicate row!

CORRECT: persist with idempotency key, then commit
  database.upsert(record, idempotencyKey);
  consumer.commitSync();
  // Reprocess is safe: upsert is a no-op on duplicate
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

For true exactly-once you need Kafka transactions that commit the output and the offset atomically, or an idempotent sink. Commit granularity matters: committing per record is slow, so you usually commit per batch, accepting that a crash reprocesses the whole batch. Handle `commitAsync` failures and do a final `commitSync` on shutdown via a `ConsumerRebalanceListener`.
